Actually, hmm, one more consideration: does the exception get thrown even with the shim's EIO? Yes, we saw it. Use try/catch. Also to be safe against other exceptions (bad_alloc — no, let that propagate).

Let me also double check: with try/catch approach, partial content discarded — yes, we return before using content.

Alternatively use the read-loop approach which mirrors FileMessageSource semantics more closely. I'll go with try/catch + bad() check; it's compact. Actually, hmm — is it guaranteed that a mid-read failure throws rather than just setting badbit silently? In libstdc++, filebuf::underflow throws on read error. In other standard libraries (libc++), it may just set... For portability, checking in.bad() after the loop covers non-throwing implementations, and the catch covers libstdc++. Good.

input.old_string:
std::ifstream in(path, std::ios::binary | std::ios::in);
if (!in) {
    return TagFileStatus::kReadError;
}
std::string content((std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(in)),
                    std::istreambuf_iterator<char>());
if (in.bad()) {
    // A read failed partway: the partial content, however complete it
    // looks, must not be used.
    return TagFileStatus::kReadError;
}

input.new_string:
std::ifstream in(path, std::ios::binary | std::ios::in);
if (!in) {
    return TagFileStatus::kReadError;
}
// Read the whole file. istreambuf_iterator reads straight from the
// streambuf, where libstdc++'s filebuf reports a failed read by
// throwing ios_base::failure (unlike istream::read, which translates
// that into badbit); catch it so a mid-read failure is the read error
// it is, not an uncaught exception. Where the implementation instead
// only sets state bits, bad() below catches the same failure.
std::string content;
try {
    content.assign(std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(in),
                   std::istreambuf_iterator<char>());
} catch (const std::ios_base::failure&) {
    return TagFileStatus::kReadError;
}
if (in.bad()) {
    // A read failed partway: the partial content, however complete it
    // looks, must not be used.
    return TagFileStatus::kReadError;
}

